Original commit body from f64e7c41eefbd53a3e3c011d71950a7c177df7c9:

WI-zisig, WI-zamum, WI-sirir, WI-sizis, WI-mitob. Five filed items, one root
cause on each side of the same contract: the schema was hand-authored where a
registry already existed.

(1) The meta blocks described 2 of 79 registered keys. axis_meta_keys
registers 32 edge-axis and 47 symbol-axis keys; Edge.meta hand-listed 2 and
Symbol.meta (a bare Dict[str, Any], so the generator emitted a contentless
{"type": "object"}) listed 0. Registered, emitted, spec-documented keys were
therefore absent from docs/schema.json: resolution_quality (1076 self-corpus
edges, WI-zisig) and channel (145 edges, WI-zamum). Both blocks now derive
their properties from the registry. Edge.meta 2 -> 34, Symbol.meta 0 -> 47.

Entries carry a description and deliberately NO type: MetaKeySpec records
none, and the values are genuinely mixed (refresh/disambiguation_fallback are
booleans, referring_paths a list, most strings), so emitting "type": "string"
across the board would turn a documentation gap into a wrong constraint.
additionalProperties stays open, so validation is unchanged and no
SCHEMA_VERSION bump is owed. The two curated entries keep their richer text.

(2) profile disagreed with its producer in both directions: it declared
total_files/total_lines, which nothing emits (WI-sizis), and omitted
dev_frameworks/framework_mode/framework_evidence, which it does (WI-sirir).
The phantom pair is REMOVED rather than populated — the same file already
records WI-bozid removing phantom lines/percentage keys from languages[*] for
this reason, and populating them would mint a second home for a derivable
fact (profile.languages[*].{files,loc}; metrics), which WI-palon declined.

(3) The spec documented a usage_contexts[].id prefix never emitted: "uc:..."
against the producer's usage:sha256:<16hex> (ir.py:1080), so a consumer
following the spec matched 0 (WI-mitob). The SPEC is corrected, not the
value: nothing in code or tests expects uc:, and churning a serialized
identity value to adopt the less readable prefix would invert WI-talos
Ruling 4.

Two ratchets: every registered key must appear in the schema for its axis,
and registry-derived entries must stay description-only with
additionalProperties open, so documenting can never become a whitelist.

